<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · 1894 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Torna a Surriento</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Neapolitan song from the 1890s that asks a loved one to come back to the seaside town of Sorrento.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Giambattista De Curtis</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Ernesto De Curtis</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Neapolitan</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Neapolitan</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Sorrento, Italy</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1894 (by tradition 1902)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Giambattista De Curtis, deposited 1894, copyrighted officially 1905)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torna-a-surriento">Torna a Surriento</a>&quot; (&quot;Come back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorrento">Sorrento</a>&quot;) is a Neapolitan song composed by the Italian musician Ernesto De Curtis to words by his brother, the poet and painter Giambattista De Curtis. Tradition holds that it was written in 1902, when Guglielmo Tramontano, the mayor of Sorrento, asked his friend Giambattista to write a song for the Prime Minister, Giuseppe Zanardelli, who was then staying at Tramontano's seaside hotel, the Imperial Hotel Tramontano; it was said to celebrate Zanardelli's stay, and some say it was a plea to him to keep his promise to help the poor town, which needed above all a sewage system. More recent research suggests the song was only reworked for the occasion: family papers show that the brothers had deposited a copy with the Italian Society of Authors and Editors in 1894, eight years before they said they wrote it. It was officially copyrighted in 1905.</p><p>It became one of the most popular songs of the Neapolitan tradition, beside &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-sole-mio">'O sole mio</a>&quot;, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funiculi-funicula">Funiculì, Funiculà</a>&quot; and &quot;Santa Lucia&quot;. Claude Aveling wrote English words for it, &quot;Come Back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorrento">Sorrento</a>&quot;, and it has been sung by performers of many kinds: the musician Bono ends his film Bono: Stories of Surrender (2025) by singing its last two verses, after remembering his father, a tenor. It was heard throughout the 1954 film Seagulls Over Sorrento, played on the concertina.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A love song set against the beauty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorrento">Sorrento</a> and its surroundings, asking a loved one to come back. The mood is longing and tender.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Neapolitan</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Vide 'o mare quant’è bello,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">spira tanto sentimento,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Comme tu a chi tiene mente,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Ca scetato 'o faje sunnà.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Guarda, gua’ chistu ciardino;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Siente, sie’ sti sciure arance:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Nu profumo accussì fino</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Dinto 'o core se ne va…</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">E tu dice: &quot;I’ parto, addio!&quot;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">T’alluntane da 'stu core…</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Da la terra de l’ammore…</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Tiene 'o core 'e nun turnà?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Ma nun me lassà,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Nun darme stu turmiento!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Torna a Surriento,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">famme campà!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Vide 'o mare de Surriento,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">che tesore tene 'nfunno:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">chi ha girato tutto 'o munno</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">nun l'ha visto comm'a ccà.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Guarda attuorno sti ssirene,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">ca te guardano 'ncantate,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">e te vonno tantu bene...</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Te vulessero vasà.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">E tu dice: &quot;I' parto, addio!&quot;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">T'alluntane da 'stu core</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Da la terra de l'ammore</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Tiene 'o core 'e nun turnà?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Ma nun me lassà,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Nun darme stu turmiento!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Torna a Surriento,</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Famme campà!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Giambattista De Curtis, the Neapolitan text as quoted on Italian Wikipedia. Text from <a href="https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torna_a_Surriento" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
